# CONTROL for selector-field.go, and the one that proves the press DOES fork.
#
# `fmt.Print("x")` reads as a type conversion. `fmt.Print("x", "y")` - the same
# bytes plus one argument - reads as a call, correctly, on both sides:
#
#   call_expression [43, 62)
#     function: selector_expression [43, 52)
#     arguments: argument_list [52, 62)   <- two literals
#
# A conversion takes exactly one operand. Add a second and the conversion
# reading has nowhere to put it, so that limb dies and the call limb is the
# only one left standing. `fmt.Print()` does the same thing from the other
# side: a conversion needs an operand and there is none.
#
# That is the whole argument that the one-argument case is a RESOLVED CONTEST
# and not a lost production. Both readings are alive in the table, both are
# carried, and the loser is picked at the end. On one argument neither dies, so
# something has to break the tie - and joints breaks it the other way from
# tree-sitter, which spends `type_conversion_expression`'s declared
# `prec.dynamic(-1)` over the finished subtrees.
roots 1
mends 0
holds call_expression
holds selector_expression
lacks type_conversion_expression
lacks qualified_type
spans call_expression 43 62
spans argument_list 52 62
